- Consumer-suite validation first exposed four orderly tests whose test helper passed no lock settings to an orderly service. Production startup always supplies Java defaults; the helper now supplies the Java `30000ms` max lock age and `20000ms` renewal interval when omitted. The consumer package suite then passed.
- The initial strict Clippy run found a collapsible conditional, a renewal mutex guard held across awaits, a complex scripted lock type, and two scripted-test guards crossing awaits. These were corrected; strict workspace Clippy passes.
- A source-path audit found existing queue renewals were applying freshness at the end of a multi-Broker batch. Lock results now retain their monotonic and wall-clock response times and apply them to the matching queue generation. Added `orderly_batch_lock_age_starts_at_each_broker_response`: with a paused clock and delayed second Broker response, the first Broker's lock expires at its own 30-second boundary while the second remains fresh.
- Added `orderly_callbacks_on_same_queue_are_serialized_in_offset_order`, which holds the first callback open and confirms a second concurrent request cannot invoke another callback until the first finishes. The independent-queue progress test proves different queues can advance in parallel.
